::Q1::What is the risk of over-reliance on AI output? 
{
=Users may accept incorrect or unsuitable output without sufficient review
~Models stop generating text
~Review becomes more accurate automatically
~AI output becomes permanently correct
}

::Q2::What type of information generally requires particular caution before being placed into an AI prompt? 
{
=Sensitive personal or confidential business information
~Publicly available generic examples only
~A generic programming keyword
~A non-sensitive formatting instruction
}

::Q3::What is bias in AI-generated content? 
{
~A type of encryption
~A guaranteed sign that every response is false
=Systematic patterns in generated output that can reflect or amplify biases in data or systems
~A method for reducing prompt length
}

::Q4::Why should AI output be treated like a first draft in many contexts? 
{
~AI output never needs editing
~First drafts are always correct
=It can be a useful starting point but still requires professional review
~A first draft is the same as verified evidence
}

::Q5::What should an organization establish for responsible AI use? 
{
=Policies describing appropriate data, disclosure, and review practices
~A rule allowing unrestricted access to every AI tool
~A rule requiring every prompt to contain confidential data
~A rule banning all verification
}